SECTION 01 — The Setup / Strategic Framing
The document positions the Web2/Web3 sequencing as "the correct commercial decision" but provides zero market validation. There is no data supporting that FCB fans in LATAM, SEA, or Africa will convert to a neobank product during the World Cup window. No conversion benchmarks, no comparable neobank launch data, no fan survey or demand signal cited. This is assertion dressed as strategy. Before $60K is committed, LFG should require Surgence to present comparable campaign data — not just ETHGas and ORBT, which are crypto-native products with crypto-native audiences, fundamentally different from a football fan neobank proposition.

SECTION 02 — Audience Strategy / Segment Table
The segmentation table has four rows but no sizing data. "800 million global fans" is cited once and never broken down. There is no addressable market estimate per segment, no conversion rate assumption, no user acquisition target per channel. A GTM document without TAM/SAM/SOM is not a strategy — it is a channel list. LFG should ask: what is the target number of neobank signups by July 19, and how does each segment contribute to that number?

SECTION 02 — Audience Strategy / Korea
Korea is elevated to a named priority segment with its own table row, but the activation plan is "local partner selection in progress." No partner is named, no methodology for selection is described, no budget is allocated. If Korea is a priority market, it should have a named partner, a confirmed budget envelope, and a launch date — not a 30-day discovery window billed at $20K/month.

SECTION 03 — Featured Growth Mechanic / Comparable Claims
The ETHGas (590K shares) and ORBT (70K shares) figures are cited as proof of concept but no context is provided. When did these campaigns run? What were the market conditions? What was the paid amplification budget behind them? What were the conversion rates from share to signup? Sharing a card is not the same as opening a bank account. The emotional mechanic may spread — but the document conflates virality with conversion, which are two entirely different outcomes. LFG should demand a conversion funnel model, not just a sharing mechanic.

SECTION 03 — Featured Growth Mechanic / CRM Data Tiers
The Tier 3 fallback (self-reported quiz) is a materially weaker product than Tier 1. The document states the launch date "does not move for data availability" — but the campaign's primary mechanic degrades significantly without real transaction history. CRM access scope should be confirmed before contract execution, not within 30 days after.

SECTION 04 — Phase 1 GTM / World Cup Window
The document states LFG has "a structural advantage" because it has closed player relationships including Messi, Yamal, Neymar, and Ronaldinho. This is a significant claim that is not substantiated anywhere in the document. Are these endorsement contracts? Content agreements? Appearance rights? What are the activation rights during the World Cup specifically — many player contracts have tournament blackout clauses with their national federations and FIFA. This needs legal confirmation before it is used as a campaign pillar.

SECTION 05 — Scope of Work / Web2 Infiltration
Every line item is an activity, not a deliverable with a measurable outcome:

Reddit — "sleeper agent placement" with no minimum post volume, engagement rate, or upvote threshold defined
Facebook — "30 regional supporter group admins briefed and activated" with no minimum reach, post frequency, or engagement per group
WhatsApp — "seeding in SEA, MENA, Africa, LATAM" with no minimum groups reached, message open rate, or referral clicks tracked
Football forums and podcasts — no minimum placements, listener reach, or impression targets
Korea — partner confirmed within 30 days but no audience size requirement, content volume, or conversion target attached

At $20K/month, Surgence can technically fulfill every line in this table by sending one WhatsApp message and briefing one admin. The scope as written has no floor. LFG should insist on a minimum performance baseline per channel before signing — or at minimum a reporting framework that defines what success looks like for each workstream by end of month 1.

SECTION 05 — Scope of Work / Phase 1 — No Delivery Timeline
The scope table lists what Surgence owns but assigns no delivery dates to individual workstreams. The only timing references in the entire document are:

Master GTM document: "within week one" (Section 04)
Loyalty Dividend brief: "within 7 days of kick-off" (Section 08)
Korea partner: "within 30 days" (Sections 05 and 08)
CRM data tier confirmation: "within 30 days" (Section 03)

Everything else — Reddit seeding, Facebook admin activation, WhatsApp seeding, forum and podcast outreach, community advisory frameworks, Telegram channel build, content governance setup — has no committed delivery date attached.
This is a material gap. May 24 is a hard deadline and Section 04 explicitly states that pre-warmed channels are the coordination advantage Surgence provides. If Reddit accounts are not seeded weeks in advance, if Facebook admins are not briefed, if WhatsApp networks have not had their 3–4 week lead time — the announcement day advantage disappears entirely.
LFG should require a detailed delivery schedule as an annex to this contract, with:

A committed go-live date per workstream
A milestone gate no later than May 10 confirming all pre-launch seeding is in place
A clear owner and sign-off process for each deliverable before May 24

Without this, Surgence can begin everything on May 23 and remain technically compliant with the contract.

SECTION 05 — Scope of Work / DAT / Institutional (Free)
"Introductions to VCs, liquidity providers, and institutional contacts" is listed as a free value-add. This is either genuinely free or it is a hook to create future fee dependency. If Surgence brokers an introduction that leads to a material institutional relationship, is there any future claim on that relationship? The document says "not a managed workstream" but does not explicitly waive any future fee or commission right on introductions made during Phase 1. This should be explicitly clarified.

SECTION 06 — Player Access / Conflict of Interest
Surgence is simultaneously running the neobank campaign and brokering sponsor deals against the LFG player roster. Every sponsor Surgence brings in is conditioned on co-marketing the neobank and committing to the $FCB token sale. This means Surgence is selecting sponsors based partly on their willingness to support Surgence's own retainer deliverables. That is a structural conflict of interest. LFG should require that sponsor selection criteria are disclosed and that LFG has approval rights over which sponsors are approached under the Player Access programme.

SECTION 06 — Player Access / Schedule B
The 60/40 split and $50K minimum deal size are noted but the $50K minimum is marked "to be confirmed" — this needs to be locked before signing. More critically, there is no clause preventing LFG from closing a deal with a sponsor that Surgence introduced outside this agreement. A standard tail clause (e.g. 12 months) on introduced parties is missing.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / Phase 1 Kill Clause
A 3-month minimum plus 30-day notice means LFG is committed to the full $60K before any exit is possible. Given that seeding and pre-registration signal should be visible within 4–6 weeks, LFG should negotiate a performance review at month 1 with a defined exit right if agreed KPIs are not met.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / Phase 2 Pre-Agreement
Locking Phase 2 at $25K/month before Phase 1 has delivered any results removes LFG's negotiating leverage entirely. The "no renegotiation" framing protects Surgence exclusively. Recommendation: Phase 2 terms are indicative only until Phase 1 milestones are confirmed, or insert a Phase 1 performance gate that must be cleared before Phase 2 auto-activates.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / Token Allocation
0.15% of $FCB supply at $500M+ FDV is potentially $750K+. Cliff and vesting are explicitly deferred to Phase 2 sign-off — meaning this negotiation happens under time pressure when TGE is imminent and Surgence's leverage is highest. Vesting structure should be agreed and written into this document now.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / Payment Structure
"Monthly in advance, USDC preferred" means LFG pays before any work is delivered each month. Combined with the 3-month minimum and no KPI floor, LFG has no financial leverage at any point in the engagement. Standard agency contracts at this fee level would include at minimum: 50% upfront / 50% on delivery of monthly milestones, or an escrow structure with milestone-based release. Payment in advance with no performance gate is entirely in Surgence's favor.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / Scope Creep Protection
The document is silent on what happens when scope expands. The Master GTM document, content governance, community advisory, and weekly sessions are all included in the $20K/month — but there is no change order process defined. If Andrew requests additional markets, additional content formats, or additional platform coverage mid-engagement, is that billable? Without a defined scope change protocol, either Surgence absorbs unlimited expansion or disputes arise. This needs a formal change order clause.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / IP Ownership
The document does not address intellectual property. The Loyalty Dividend mechanic, the GTM document, the messaging framework, the moderation playbooks, the content calendar — who owns these at the end of the engagement? If LFG terminates after month 3, can Surgence reuse the Loyalty Dividend architecture for another client? Can they pitch the same mechanic to a competing football club or neobank? IP ownership and exclusivity terms are entirely absent.

SECTION 07 — Commercial Terms / Exclusivity
Is Surgence exclusive to LFG in the football/neobank category during Phase 1 and Phase 2? The document is silent. If Surgence is simultaneously running a campaign for a competing football club token or a competing neobank product, the "no token signal" protocol and the seeding networks are directly compromised. Category exclusivity should be a standard clause at this fee level.

GENERAL — Governance and Reporting
The document mentions "weekly alignment sessions" but defines no formal reporting structure. There is no committed reporting cadence, no dashboard, no weekly metrics pack, no monthly performance review format. For a campaign running across Reddit, WhatsApp, Facebook, podcasts, Korea, and a viral product mechanic simultaneously, the absence of a defined reporting framework means LFG is flying blind. A top-tier agency engagement at this fee level would include: weekly performance dashboard, monthly written report against KPIs, and a defined escalation process if targets are missed.